So, what Aspen speaks about is not the historical Moses, who Moses really was, but the memory that we have of Moses. And that is what is significant both in rabbinical literature, in the scripture, in my monotheists, and in Aspen.

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:42.000
So, I do see that Aspen, from my point of view, again, the contribution is to say, really, I'm not interested in who Moses really, really, really was.

00:41:42.000 --> 00:42:03.000
What I'm saying is, the memory, yes, of Moses. I can say, you speak Arabic, I can say, the afar that Moses left us, you see, the traces that Moses left us.

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:13.000
And that is why I think that there are three disciplines or areas in the question.

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:28.000
And now, three are about the interpretation of my monotheist. As far as I know, my monotheist is the distinction between intellect and imagination, which is very major in Europe.

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:51.000
As far as I know, my monotheist is the definition of these two capacities. My monotheist defines intellect as a power of reason, as a power that reflects reality, while he defines imagination as a power that creates new combinations.

00:42:51.000 --> 00:43:06.000
As a matter of fact, the creative power, the creative capacity. Now, he doesn't mention this creativity as something of wrong syllogism, of taking the predicate instead of the subject.

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:23.000
His example is proper creative. Sfinat Balzev after Baavir, an iron boat running in the air, taking a few predicates from different objects and combining them together.

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:43.000
Why do I think it is important? It is important because it reflects also the conception of man and philosophy. When he says that the first two commandments are intellectual, that is the point.

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:58.000
But it is not that he means that they are rational. It means that they refer to the intellect. They are addressed to the intellect. The commander, the commanding God, says to the intellect what to believe in.

00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:20.000
It is not that the intellect is the one that grasps it, but the intellect is the one that is supposed to obey. While on the other hand, the more normative, the more practical commandments are such that there is nothing that reflects reality in them, and that is why the prophet needs creativity in order to invent them.

00:44:20.000 --> 00:44:31.000
And that is why Maimonides calls them Kusumot, conventional, based on convention, not on intellect. And I think it drops some of the points you raise in this.

00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:46.000
What you express is the imagination of Maimonides, or the conventional understanding of Maimonides, but that is not what Maimonides says. Let me explain why.

00:44:46.000 --> 00:45:12.000
Maimonides says that the prophets are superior to the philosophers because of the prophets' imagination.

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:33.000
All right? Now, be very careful when you read Maimonides' Catholic. You see, when imagination, what you call reality, my imagination is the one that interprets sensory experience, you call reality experience.

00:45:34.000 --> 00:45:56.000
And then, after interpreting sensory experience through imagination, we best reason about our reason is grounded on this sensory imagination apparatus. We will be wrong.

00:45:57.000 --> 00:46:17.000
The way to do it, when Maimonides speaks about the prophets, is different. What you call reality. Sensory experience is processed through reason, and then you apply imagination, and imagination takes you further.

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:35.000
Let me give an example, which I mentioned in my opening. We see things fly. We want to fly. How to fly? Well, we could approach it through reason or through imagination.

00:46:36.000 --> 00:47:02.000
Let's do it through imagination. We see a bird. It has wings. Yes. It has feathers. Yes. It has a back. And therefore, I would dress like a bird, with feathers, and go to Italy, Pisa Tower, or New York, Manhattan, Empire State Building, and jump.

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:23.000
What happens? I process reality through imagination, predicates. A bird flies. A bird has wings. I have wings, therefore I fly. That's one way. Ma, I'm Superman, and the child jumps.

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:45.000
Another way is to understand the principle of aerodynamics. That is reason. You process flying through reason. If you process flying through reason, you will discover the principle of aerodynamics.

00:47:45.000 --> 00:48:06.000
Now, aerodynamics is wonderful, but if you don't have the imagination of an engineer, you're never going to build an airplane. So, therefore, you have Ma, I fly, sensory perception, imagination, jumping, Empire State Building.

00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:31.000
All of you have the Wright Brothers, sensory perception, rationality, and F-16, or whatever it is, which is the extension of this principle through creative imagination. But that imagination is based upon a reality that was processed by reason.

00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:41.000
I hope one very... No, no, please, I enjoy this.

00:48:41.000 --> 00:48:56.000
It doesn't regard the overall pieces, but just one point of distinguishing between HaShem Elokim and HaShem Avayad, or the name God and the name of the Tetragrammaton.

00:48:56.000 --> 00:49:21.000
I think the distinction is, once again, it's different. It's two lingual means of referring to God. One is, as a matter of fact, it parallels quite well the modern distinction between a proper name, calling someone in his name, as an individual,

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:38.000
and, what Russell and Frege say, that every proper name is a shortened form of a definite description, a definitizing description. Instead of calling him in his name, you can say, the man who did such and such and such things, or has such and such qualities.

00:49:38.000 --> 00:49:54.000
And, as a matter of fact, once again, it seems, from Yehuda Levy, that he sees it the same way, that the proper name is just a shortened form of the overall description, and not a way of just referring to the same individual.

00:49:54.000 --> 00:50:14.000
But that is the effect of reading Yehuda Levy and other medieval commentators who are knowing Arabic, and this allows you to make a certain kind of exegesis which is supported by the text. The text is like this. Yehuda Levy, Maimonides, Rabbi Simchaon, before him, also Carvite.

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:39.000
This gentleman, Abu Shihada, yes? You understand Arabic well, I suppose. He says, the name of God, Adonai, is Alam Al-Taluri, Bil-Shahada. Do you have an experience, Shahada?

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:55.000
Yes? I know Arabic very well. I know what Shahada means. It means mystical and non-mystical. Yes? And a Shaheed could be, I remember a witness coming to a tribunal saying that my next door neighbor's dog ruined my garden.

00:50:56.000 --> 00:51:23.000
I didn't say Mushahada, I said Shaheed, Shahada. Yes? So, God, Yud Kebakeh, appeals, reveals. Once that He reveals, you acquire something which, I don't like the word because in English it has different connotations,

00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:38.000
we have an intuitive, it means something, an element that we like better. It's something that moves you. And this knowledge, you acquire it through an experience.

00:51:38.000 --> 00:52:06.000
Elohim is something that you know, not because you had a personal experience, like 'Am Yisrael Behal Sinai. Yes? But you thought, you think, and you come to a conclusion that there is or there must be one or many Elohim.

00:52:06.000 --> 00:52:23.000
Yes? Elohim, it's insofar as number singular or plural, is neutral. So, you have an experience. And then, I like it the way Shahada goes on, that it is al-ma'bud, to worship.

00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:45.000
But it's not, again, it's an anthropocentric God. Whether the God that reveals Himself to him and allows you to perceive Him. Yes? You are, you have a certain kind of knowledge inside you and that moves you to certain things.

00:52:45.000 --> 00:53:03.000
So, again, by the way, I deal with, what does it mean, name? What does it mean, name? Israel, when Moses sees God in the burning bush, he says, ah, I've got to go over there to the Jewish people and I've got to tell them God made me.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:18.000
And by Yom ha-Kippurim we must say more, ma'uma'aleh. That was the question, what is His name? What should I answer? Now, the question asked by my mother, etc., etc., one minute, do they know the name?

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:41.000
They don't know the name? Google, what does it mean, name, in Hebrew? That's why I think it's wrong to project Prague, etc., etc., into a Semitic text.

00:53:42.000 --> 00:54:02.000
In Hebrew, it's the father. If you walk in the street and somebody says, Benny, what happened? You look, what do you want? Shem means the possibility to address somebody. There is a God, what is His telephone number?

00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:16.000
They don't know. Ma shemo? Ma'uma'aleh? You want to call Him? I want to speak to God. And He gives the answer.

00:54:16.000 --> 00:54:41.000
And that is the way when we want to speak to God, we dial this number. How do we address God in the 'Amida?

00:54:41.000 --> 00:55:01.000
Eloheinu, Elohehavotenu, Elohe Abraham, Elohe Yisrael, Elohe Yaaqob. So, name of God means that you could call Him and establish a dialogue with Him. That is within a Semitic text.

00:55:02.000 --> 00:55:27.000
I would argue with your repudiation of the Jewish festivals. You're saying that they're not renewing your only existing historical time, incorrect? And that they're not participating in the same kind of pagan, Eviadian, Igotempus, renewal, creation event.

00:55:27.000 --> 00:55:42.000
But I would argue with that in that I think that the festivals do have a kind of restorative quality in that they renew our covenant in the Berit. We talked about that in your paper as being important.

00:55:42.000 --> 00:56:06.000
For example, Shabu'ot. On Shabu'ot, I have to put myself in the position of Ma'amat HaOtsinai. I have to participate in Ma'amat HaOtsinai all over again. On Kesav we're going to read the Haggadah. I have to participate in the Etiad misva. I have to teach my children that they themselves have to experience Etiad misva because they have to go back to that event.

00:56:06.000 --> 00:56:29.000
And that event, therefore, even though it is a historical event, it becomes a primordial historical event. It becomes in our culture, even though it is a historical culture, because I have to put myself in that position, then I relate to it in the same way as a pagan culture relates to a kind of, like the Eucharist.

00:56:29.000 --> 00:56:36.000
It's the same kind of cyclical Eliadean restorative event. Immediately.

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:11.000
You eat from the Rabbi, and you become, the whole group becomes eclectically united as with the Eucharist. I have nothing against it. It's very, very nice.

00:57:12.000 --> 00:57:32.000
But that is not the meaning of Zecher. When I take a glass of wine, I say the Kiddush. I am fulfilling the misva of Zecher LeMaas Ebershid. Now, believe it or not, I don't remember anything about Ma'as Ebershid. I was too little when Ma'as Ebershid took place.

00:57:33.000 --> 00:57:36.000
So what does it mean, Zecher LeMaas Ebershid? I'll tell you what it means.

00:57:36.000 --> 00:57:38.000
No, but it means that you have to...

00:57:38.000 --> 00:57:55.000
You have to let me finish. I'll tell you what it means. It means like this. My preceding generation passed me a bowl of Ma'as Ebershid, and I'm going to pass the bowl to the next generation, to my children at the glass.

00:57:55.000 --> 00:58:12.000
This is passing the bowl, yes? And that's all what it means. When the Seadat Ha'on very properly, and Rambam very properly, say that Begat Ester, with the meaning of Begat Ester, you fulfill the misva of Zachor Asher Lecha Asa HaMalek.

00:58:12.000 --> 00:58:18.000
There is nothing. The word Hamalek does not appear there. This word Agadir, whatever that may mean.

00:58:19.000 --> 00:58:36.000
And what it means, therefore? Remembering doesn't mean to remember. No. There's a message that was passed, and the message changes to contemporary time-space continuum.

00:58:36.000 --> 00:58:52.000
If I'm going to remember like people 3,000 years ago, I have to be 3,000 years ago. There's a fantastic story. When I was teaching at JPS, I had my students read the story.

00:58:52.000 --> 00:59:07.000
It is by, I think it's translated into Hebrew, but I'm sure it's translated into English, by Borges. Paul Menard, author of Don Quixote. Fantastic story. Somebody in Spain, no, I'm sorry, in France.

00:59:07.000 --> 00:59:31.000
Somebody in France wants to write a Quixote. How does he want to write a Quixote? By becoming Cervantes. And therefore, he automatically would write a Don Quixote.

00:59:31.000 --> 00:59:53.000
Of course, what Borges shows, this is impossible. It's only the effect of some kind of intellectual neurosis. Now, you cannot go into the past. Time is linear. You could fantasize that you're in the past. Fine.

00:59:53.000 --> 01:00:15.000
Fine. Again, we're going into mythical thinking. Yes? And this is possible. But if time is linear, if there is really history, it means that I can never understand the past.

01:00:15.000 --> 01:00:30.000
What can happen is that the past, because it was a special kind of people, send me some messages that I will have to decode today in my time-space continuum.

01:00:45.000 --> 01:01:07.000
And God will answer us by the power of Moses. God will answer us by the power of Moses. God will answer us through the possibility of Moses. And therefore, he passes this to others.

01:01:07.000 --> 01:01:29.000
And we do it, we have to translate that experience, yes? Not by science fiction traveling into the past, by translating this into the space-time continuum in which I have been living.

01:01:29.000 --> 01:01:49.000
Just a small question. If we relate to the first two commandments as a message of rational thinking, shouldn't they come after the process of four years in the desert, not Bereshit?

01:01:49.000 --> 01:02:13.000
It's a very good question. I think it's a very good question. My mind has another word. I'm so happy that somebody here knows Arabic. Of course, at the beginning, there is something new. Yes, there is God in the dimension.

01:02:13.000 --> 01:02:33.000
And we perceive it, my mind, I don't know, somehow it's programmed differently. And then, here's what my mind called Luther. Luther is a very special kind of word. He's a guidance, but he's a special kind of guidance.

01:02:33.000 --> 01:02:51.000
Now, I'm going to, because you speak Arabic, I presume, I'm going to tell you something about the Arabic of the Jews. In the Arabic of the Jews, you could say something like this. You could say, Luther, of course, is a guidance, and he's nice, but also you could say, Luther for Kash.

01:02:51.000 --> 01:03:19.000
Yes, of course, but Luther for Kash. Luther for Kash. What does it mean? God sometimes hit us, of course, gently, not to destroy us, through the desert, because we know the principle.

01:03:19.000 --> 01:03:44.000
There is a God. And the 40 years in the desert, and I deal with this in my own music, because is the Luther of God, yes, like the person teaching the little boy how to read, to expose him to the other bed, and then through Luther here and there, he guides the person so he is able to read on his own.

01:03:45.000 --> 01:04:04.000
So, my mind is, speaks about this Luther, yes, and therefore, first you have to know the principles, in theory, and then the good teacher guides you, yes, the guidance that comes.

01:04:04.000 --> 01:04:28.000
Yes, and there's a beautiful word that says, Shajar. The person, after, as a result of this 40 years of here and there, the person acquires Geburah. But it's not Geburah in the sense of what I'm about, but of character, of personality, of chutzpah.

01:04:28.000 --> 01:04:36.000
I'm going to do it. I'm going to do it. I'm going to do it. So, this is the way my mind sees the 40 years in the desert.

01:04:36.000 --> 01:04:53.000
My question is, during your immediate reading of Freud, I mean, I know it's Freud, but I don't know...

01:05:06.000 --> 01:05:25.000
Again, I would say that is, and I don't mean it in a narrow way, a Semitic kind of mysticism. Not agnostic mysticism, which is Greek or Hellenistic, but a Semitic.

01:05:25.000 --> 01:05:40.000
When I see it, I tell you what I see. I don't know if you read, I don't know what it says in Hebrew, I don't know what it's published in Israel, even in Arabic. This is a point that I think has to be studied to understand that kind of a thing.

01:05:40.000 --> 01:06:03.000
And there was, I think there was, this kind of mysticism. And what is important is that when you read the children of my mother's works, like his son Abraham, his grandson Yeshua, and so on, all these books, they are very much within that tradition.

01:06:03.000 --> 01:06:23.000
So, I don't see my mind as different so much of, alright, let me put it, I think that my mind is much more different than the qabbala, than even Sina or Al-Ghazali. That is for sure.

01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:42.000
That is the point. So on this I do concur with you. There is a different kind of mysticism, and I don't know whether, I presume they are studied in Israel, yes? I don't know whether at universities, they do teach?

01:06:42.000 --> 01:07:04.000
Yeah, yeah. So this is, the answer is yes. And then you asked me a question about a Freudian reading of my book. I'm a meridian reading of Freud. I'm a Sepharadi Jew, coming from Spain originally, and there is a kind of a conversal tradition.

01:07:04.000 --> 01:07:30.000
Conversal tradition means you live in a society, you know the language, but you know that you cannot write what you think. That's the conversal tradition. Because of this, I understand the German-Austrian tradition in the 19th century as essentially a conversal tradition. Jews wrote in German, but they could not say what they thought.

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:46.000
Alright? So my reading of 19th century German-Jewish literature is parallel to my reading of Spanish conversal literature. That's point number one.

01:07:46.000 --> 01:08:08.000
In my introduction, I point out that there are many elements in Freud. You should read it carefully. The criticism is not against religion, but against Christian religion. Yes?

01:08:08.000 --> 01:08:28.000
And if you are just careful, just read what he says. You discover that the Moses of Freud is Jesus. And the people who killed that Moses and ate him up, yes, are not the Jews.

01:08:28.000 --> 01:08:48.000
Why he didn't write Jesus and Christianity? Because if you were to write about Jesus and Christianity, you would not have heard of Freud today. Alright? The same way that all conversals wanted to write something, they had a special kind.

01:08:48.000 --> 01:09:10.000
Yes, he makes a point. So I do not see Freud as a person rejecting Jewish religion. As a matter of fact, I think my reading of Freud is different. I think you could read it in my introduction.

01:09:10.000 --> 01:09:28.000
People like Jean-Paul Sartre was very convinced that all these Jews had told him that when they were criticizing Christianity, a religion, they didn't have in their mind Judaism, but they had in their mind Christianity. And this is what he said. Yes?

01:09:28.000 --> 01:09:52.000
But, again, he was in a situation in which in the 1900s, to be more explicit about it, would have been... So that says, I think, we have to read carefully what he says and to see whether the description of the facts fit which religion fit.

01:09:52.000 --> 01:10:20.000
And when you do that, then you see that... Don't pick Judaism. And it's not a criticism of culture, but it's a criticism of Christian culture. Yes? And, again, not of religion, but of, not even Christian, I would say, Catholic religion.

01:10:20.000 --> 01:10:43.000
And that is my interpretation of Freud. That's why I say we should have, at my beginning, reading of Freud. Assume that Zemlin-Freud read my marriage, see it through the eyes of my marriage, and then you see that he's criticizing not religion, not Buddhism, not Islam, not Protestant religion. He's criticizing Catholic religion. That's my opinion.

01:10:44.000 --> 01:10:46.000
Do you have a question?

01:10:46.000 --> 01:10:48.000
Yeah, yeah.

01:10:48.000 --> 01:10:50.000
I was thinking to become...

01:10:50.000 --> 01:10:52.000
Oh.

01:10:52.000 --> 01:11:00.000
...religious study of modern rational thought. Do you mean also that?

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:13.000
No, I mean that to understand imagination. You see, the work, if I would have to translate it... Unfortunately, the guide was translated by people who do not know either English or Arabic. And I say with all due respect.

01:11:13.000 --> 01:11:34.000
Penis was a great Arabist, but he didn't know English. I mean, I write English, and some people think that my English is fantastic, blah, blah, blah. But a regular American, an English philosopher, opens Penis' Guide to the Prophecy. I mean, that's not English.

01:11:35.000 --> 01:11:48.000
The best translation, by the way, was, and still is, Salomon Munch, the French translation. Because if you know French, you could read it. You could agree, disagree, but you could read it.

01:11:48.000 --> 01:12:05.000
So, but unfortunately, the guide has been translated into English, and so that's why I use the word imagination. But, you know, I thought that instead of imagination, I should use the word for Chayal, illusion. And therefore, the future of an illusion...

01:12:05.000 --> 01:12:06.000
Wahem.

01:12:06.000 --> 01:12:07.000
What?

01:12:07.000 --> 01:12:08.000
Wahem.

01:12:08.000 --> 01:12:09.000
No.

01:12:09.000 --> 01:12:10.000
Chayal?

01:12:10.000 --> 01:12:24.000
No. Again, my argument is not with Wahem and Chayal, but what means imagination in English, and what are the semantic aspects of illusion.

01:12:25.000 --> 01:12:49.000
So, anyhow, I wrote it, imagination. I was, you know, but I, it's an imagination that leads you, or permits you to live in a kind of a bubble, yes, and to perceive things in ways which do not accommodate to reality.

01:12:49.000 --> 01:12:59.000
Again, permits you to believe that you are Superman because your mommy bought you a $5 Superman suit.

01:12:59.000 --> 01:13:06.000
Yeah, all right. And in English, I would like, I think that imagination...

01:13:06.000 --> 01:13:07.000
Or Chayal.

01:13:07.000 --> 01:13:08.000
Yeah.

01:13:08.000 --> 01:13:13.000
[inaudible]

01:13:13.000 --> 01:13:17.000
No. No, I don't think so. I say that imagination...

01:13:17.000 --> 01:13:18.000
No, no, I...

01:13:18.000 --> 01:13:29.000
I give an example of imagination, but I've been in my, I know that you were waiting, and if you forgive me, I will address this.

01:13:29.000 --> 01:13:33.000
Let me give you an example of imagination.

01:13:33.000 --> 01:13:36.000
I met an anthropologist. You are. I mean, some of you are.

01:13:36.000 --> 01:13:47.000
Suppose, I don't know, I'm an explorer going to some, you know, remote village in, I don't know, let's make it interesting, in the Amazon.

01:13:47.000 --> 01:13:48.000
All right?

01:13:48.000 --> 01:13:58.000
I go there, and they see me, and they see that I have a blue in my shirt.

01:13:59.000 --> 01:14:03.000
Only yesterday, they saw a bird, X.

01:14:03.000 --> 01:14:05.000
Good or bad omen.

01:14:05.000 --> 01:14:13.000
And that bird has blue feathers or blue eyes.

01:14:13.000 --> 01:14:16.000
Predicate, predicate.

01:14:16.000 --> 01:14:18.000
He has a blue shirt.

01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:21.000
The bird has blue eyes or blue feathers.

01:14:21.000 --> 01:14:26.000
Therefore, A equals B.

01:14:26.000 --> 01:14:28.000
And therefore, I would be predicated with A.

01:14:28.000 --> 01:14:29.000
And A equals 1.

01:14:29.000 --> 01:14:31.000
That is imagination.

01:14:31.000 --> 01:14:47.000
Now, that kind of imagination makes you process reality in terms that do not correspond to the laws of physics, when used to objective reality.

01:14:47.000 --> 01:14:52.000
You have perceived, processed already, your vision, yes?

01:14:52.000 --> 01:14:57.000
Since this blue has an omen for something.

01:14:57.000 --> 01:15:08.000
And therefore, in that sense, puerhayaire is bordering into fantasy, into illusion.

01:15:08.000 --> 01:15:16.000
But, again, you know, let me give another better example of fantasy.

